---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SectionBackdrop from '../components/SectionBackdrop.astro';
import { getHaloArtDirectionImages } from '../lib/art-direction-gallery';
import { RESUME_URL, RESUME_LABEL } from '../config/features';

const backdrop = getHaloArtDirectionImages();

const principles = [
  {
    title: 'Leverage the past to build the future',
    body: 'The quickest way to lose a beloved franchise is to prove you can change it. Leading art on Halo Infinite, I anchored the direction in legacy — mining the franchise for the silhouettes, materials, and moments players remembered — and only expanded once that foundation felt secure. Chasing “better than before” had quietly eroded trust; restoring what was sacred is what earned us the room to innovate. Reverence isn’t nostalgia — it’s the ground you stand on to reach further.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Vision has to survive production',
    body: 'A visual target only matters if it holds up at gameplay distance, in a live build, months after the pitch deck is closed. I set direction teams can actually execute, and I hold a hard line on readability: if it doesn’t read at ten or twenty feet, it doesn’t need to be built. Beautiful frames are easy; a look that ships — and keeps shipping — is the craft.',
  },
  {
    title: 'At scale, the work is alignment',
    body: 'A franchise isn’t made by one hand — it’s hundreds, across parallel productions and seasons of live content that all have to read as one world. On Halo Infinite I authored the Art Index, a living style guide that gave a ~400-artist program and our external partners one vocabulary and one bar. Cohesion beats any hero asset: how everything sits together in-game matters more than how good a single piece looks in isolation.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Live service is a creative discipline, not a treadmill',
    body: 'Seasons, events, earnables, and commerce are how a modern world stays alive — and where art meets the business most directly. I treat cadence and monetization as craft, not overhead: every drop should deepen the fiction, reward the people who love it, and respect their time and their wallet. Done well, commerce is world-building that funds the next chapter.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Lead the people first',
    body: 'Teams make the work; my job is to make the team. That means hiring for range, mentoring toward a clear bar, protecting focus from the churn, and giving artists enough trust to surprise me. The art is downstream of a healthy, aligned, well-led group of people — so that’s where I spend my attention.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Perspective"
  description="Darren Bacon on stewarding beloved worlds — how he leads art for franchises at scale, from vision through live-service and commerce."
>
  <section class="relative isolate overflow-hidden border-b border-border/60">
    <SectionBackdrop images={backdrop} opacity={0.14} />
    <div class="relative z-10 mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-24 lg:px-8" data-reveal>
      <p class="text-sm tracking-[0.2em] uppercase text-accent">Point of view</p>
      <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-5xl">On stewarding beloved worlds</h1>
      <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted">
        How I think about leading art for franchises people love.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-16 lg:px-8">
    <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed text-fg/90" data-reveal>
      A beloved game world is a kind of trust. Players don’t just buy it — they move in. They learn its
      light, its silhouettes, the way its metal catches the sun. A director’s job isn’t to reinvent that
      world, nor to freeze it in amber. It’s to evolve it so that every year it feels more like itself.
      Holding those two forces in tension — reverence and momentum — is the whole job.
    </p>

    <aside class="mt-10 rounded-2xl border border-accent/30 bg-accent/5 p-6 sm:p-8" data-reveal>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-fg/80 sm:text-base">
        This isn’t theory. On <span class="font-medium text-fg">Halo Infinite</span> I authored the
        <span class="font-medium text-fg">Art Index</span> — the franchise’s living art style guide —
        codifying the pillars and stewardship philosophy that aligned a ~400-artist program and its
        external partners around a single vision.
      </p>
    </aside>

    <div class="mt-14 space-y-12">
      {
        principles.map((item, index) => (
          <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-[auto_1fr] sm:gap-8" data-reveal data-reveal-stagger>
            <span class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-accent/40 tabular-nums">
              {String(index + 1).padStart(2, '0')}
            </span>
            <div>
              <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">{item.title}</h2>
              <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted">{item.body}</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <p class="mt-16 border-t border-border/60 pt-10 text-xl leading-relaxed text-fg/90" data-reveal>
      Stewarding a franchise at this scale is the most demanding version of this job — and it’s the one I
      want: evolve the look, honor the promise, grow the people, and move the business, all at once, and
      all in service of a world players never want to leave.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-10 flex flex-wrap gap-4" data-reveal>
      <a
        href="/contact"
        class="inline-flex items-center rounded-full bg-accent px-6 py-3 text-sm font-medium tracking-wide text-bg transition hover:bg-accent-dim"
      >
        Start a conversation
      </a>
      <a
        href={RESUME_URL}
        class="inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border px-6 py-3 text-sm font-medium tracking-wide text-fg transition hover:border-accent hover:text-accent"
      >
        {RESUME_LABEL}
      </a>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
